gh issue create --title "The contact form discards the error it catches, so a failed submission leaves nothing to diagnose" --body 'Found by `code-review` during #286, in a file that change does not touch.

## Measured

`apps/web/src/components/contact-form.tsx:44` binds the caught error and never reads it:

```js
} catch (err) {
  setError("Something went wrong. Please try again.");
}
```

`err` is discarded entirely. Nothing is logged, nothing is reported, and the user-facing string is a constant. So a network failure, a JSON parse failure and a 500 from `/api/contact` are indistinguishable from each other in the browser console, in the container logs, and to the person reporting the bug.

## Why it is distinct from #326

#326 is about the error message not being *announced* — the rendered `<p>` carries no `role="alert"` and no live region, so assistive technology never learns the submission failed. That is about telling the user.

This is about the failure never being recorded anywhere at all. Fixing #326 would give a screen reader the same constant string this issue is about, and would still leave nobody able to say why the submission failed. They want fixing together but they are not the same defect.

## Suggested fix

Log the caught error before replacing it with the user-facing string, so the diagnostic survives:

```js
} catch (err) {
  console.error("Contact form submission failed", err);
  setError("Something went wrong. Please try again.");
}
```

Whether it should go further — distinguishing a network failure from a server error in what the user is told — is a product question. The minimum is that the original error stops vanishing.

## Worth knowing when fixing

`ui-review` exercised this path during #286 by forcing a 500 from `/api/contact` and found a third problem in the same handler, which belongs with whichever change picks this up: `isSubmitting` disables the submit button, which blurs it, so when the error renders `document.activeElement` is `<body>`. A keyboard user is returned to the top of the document with no indication anything happened. That is recorded in #326 rather than here, since it is about the user learning of the failure.

## Not established

Whether any other handler in the app discards a caught error the same way. Only this one was measured.' 2>&1 | tail -1

